#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135120 :tutorial/content "The following two vector fields behave either as a source or a sink. Therefore, they both have divergence.\n\n![](images/93/939efa85ebfe4805a27595b60a797b8a3435075590ee77f7a18d04bd76d91f1a.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are solenoidal (i.e., they each have zero divergence).\n\n![](images/ed/edb2f908fa78439bf985b5754c9fcc5968c21926dbe124297b67af046a9f70a3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ddbe7ae2-caaf-5436-b1c7-aabc0eec115d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5793 :tutorial/title "Examples of Vector Field With Divergence and Solenoidal Vector Fields"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135121 :tutorial/content "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "9b8de083-51c2-581b-ad36-a773dca8af05" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5794 :tutorial/title "Some Important Notes About the Divergence of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaebc78-d8b2-5500-9c52-f82bd3d3a8a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5839 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\left(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5840 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135133 :tutorial/content "We now know how to calculate the circulation of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over a region $D$ that contains holes. \n\nIn previous lessons, we've seen how to use Green's theorem to calculate circulation. Often, Green's theorem allows us to convert a complex line integral over $\\partial D$ to a double integral over $D.$\n\nSo the question is, can we use Green's theorem to calculate the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ when $D$ contains holes?\n\nAs it turns out, the answer is yes. Moreover, we can show that applying Green's theorem to regions containing holes works the same way as with regions that don't have holes. We'll describe this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nWe can summarize Green's theorem to regions containing holes in a single picture, shown below. \n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNote that the region $D$ can contain a finite number of holes." :tutorial/id #uuid "50e81026-984c-52b1-8e07-d3af0248f817" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5846 :tutorial/title "Extending Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135134 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that we want to calculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_\\limits{D} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region shown below. \n\n![](images/65/65f622409e3fdd43f56dde79513e8693751465bb131dd828ce9f6cdfaa7d48aa.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the region $D$ can be represented as the difference of sets $D = \\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 4, -2 \\leq y \\leq 3 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d5a2750-408d-5d86-9d7b-2c95f46f262b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5847 :tutorial/title "A Note About Double Integrals Over Regions With Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135135 :tutorial/content "Technically, we can only find the curl of a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then (strictly speaking) $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ has no meaning. \n\nHowever, it's sometimes helpful to avoid this issue by considering $\\mathbf F$ as a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ whose $\\mathbf k$ component is zero everywhere. \n\nAssuming that the $\\mathbf k$ component of $\\mathbf F$ is zero everywhere, the curl must point in the $z$-direction perpendicular to your screen. To see why, note that if we start from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k,\n$$\n\nthen set \n\n$$\nR(x,y,z) = 0, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z}= \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} = 0\n$$\n\nand, finally, substitute the above into our curl formula, then we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \n= \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nNote that the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\n$$\n\n \nis called the **scalar curl** of $\\mathbf F.$ The scalar curl is associated with two-dimensional vector fields only." :tutorial/id #uuid "07a27175-fb76-5663-98dd-203ead020fc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5848 :tutorial/title "The Curl of a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5c61d2a3-2e54-5e8b-931c-beff07f3581e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5857 :tutorial/title "Negatively Oriented Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135138 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below. \n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ and has a hole. \n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D.$ So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma \n= \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n+\n\\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2.$ For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c36205a2-23f5-5757-a4ad-9c15c9db94a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5858 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135139 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem. \n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D.$\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D,$ we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "1905ba8e-41ed-5562-9da9-28cae31e9c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5859 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Choice of Orentations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135140 :tutorial/content "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700e03f-e7d7-5f2c-88cb-17e9c69b7ed5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5860 :tutorial/title "An Illustration of How To Extend Green’s Theorem to Regions Containing Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135160 :tutorial/content "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\n    \n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." :tutorial/id #uuid "6855af2b-f345-5f4d-aae2-49d6ee758d9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135164 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e318cc2d-81e3-5cbd-b0ce-2f076bfd06b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7732 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Flux Across a Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135170 :tutorial/content "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n*Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca1c4682-71c7-5950-950a-a33ad1e84c23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7773 :tutorial/title "The Divergence Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152158 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151947} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720174} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720173}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151948} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720370} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720409}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153354 :tutorial/content "To calculate the line integral of a function $f(x,y)$ along a curve $C,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ must be parametrized by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ to apply this formula.\n\nNow suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y}{x} \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $y = x^2$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n![](images/a8/a8a3fe6c05479258f84aa074398e92a8a849fa4d015feb450aeef6a6066a870e.png)\n\nTo reduce the calculation of the line integral to a parametric case, we must first parametrize the curve.\n\nNotice that the curve $C$ is of the form $y = y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) =  t^2.$ This gives the following parametrization for $C\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, t^2 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 1.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({x}^{'}(t))}^{2}+{({y}^{'}(t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, using $x=t$ and $y=t^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the line integral in terms of $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e899dbe-9337-5b9c-8fb7-d4d061abf999" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564599} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157300 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}]}
